---
<span id="abstract" class="section-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="project-section surgery-section" id="background" aria-label="Minimally invasive surgery">
  <p class="surgery-lead">Minimally invasive surgery requires precise manipulation, reliable visual feedback, and adaptive control in confined and dynamic anatomical environments. Our research investigates how robotics and artificial intelligence can enable more intelligent, adaptable, and reliable surgical assistance.</p>
  <div class="surgery-single"><Figure id="surgical-workspace" quiet /></div>
</section>

<section class="project-section surgery-section surgery-challenges" id="problems" aria-labelledby="problems-title">
  <h2 id="problems-title">Three Research Challenges</h2>
  <div class="surgery-direction-grid">
    {challenges.map(item => <article class="surgery-direction">
      <p class="surgery-number">{item.number}</p>
      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
      <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> {item.challenge}</p>
      <p class="surgery-direction-label"><strong>Research Direction:</strong></p>
      <a href={`#${item.target}`}>{item.direction}<span aria-hidden="true"> ↓</span></a>
    </article>)}
  </div>
</section>

<span id="innovations" class="section-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="project-section surgery-section" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
  <h2 id="method-title">01 — Magnetically Actuated Surgical Cameras</h2>
  <p class="surgery-lead">We investigate magnetic actuation mechanisms for flexible positioning and orientation of internal surgical cameras. By coupling an external robotic actuator with an internal camera, this approach explores alternative methods for controlling surgical viewpoints within constrained workspaces.</p>
  <h3 class="surgery-subhead">External Magnetic Actuation</h3>
  <p class="surgery-sublead">A laboratory prototype explores robotic magnetic actuation, while mechanical schematics and animations illustrate the proposed actuator–camera coupling and camera motion.</p>
  <div class="surgery-pair surgery-actuation">
    <div><p class="surgery-media-label">Laboratory prototype</p><Video id="bench-prototype" /></div>
    <div><p class="surgery-media-label">Actuator–camera coupling (animation)</p><Video id="camera-guidance" /></div>
  </div>
  <p class="surgery-media-label surgery-media-label-wide">Mechanical schematic</p>
  <div class="surgery-wide surgery-schematic"><Figure id="surgery-mechanism" quiet /></div>
</section>

<section class="project-section surgery-section" id="cleaning" aria-labelledby="cleaning-title">
  <h2 id="cleaning-title">02 — Bioinspired Optical Maintenance</h2>
  <p class="surgery-lead">Inspired by the protective and cleaning mechanisms of the human eye, we investigate bioinspired robotic systems for maintaining endoscopic visibility. The proposed approach integrates robotic wiping, compliant fluid delivery, and perception-guided control to address optical contamination.</p>
  <div class="surgery-wide"><Figure id="eye-concept" quiet /></div>
  <div class="surgery-pair surgery-cleaning-parts">
    <div><h3>Bioinspired Robotic Eyelid</h3><p class="surgery-part-text">A shape-memory-alloy-driven wiping mechanism is proposed to remove contaminants from the camera’s optical surface.</p><Figure id="eyelid-concept" quiet /></div>
    <div><h3>Soft Robotic Cleaning Nozzle</h3><p class="surgery-part-text">A compliant nozzle integrates air and liquid channels to direct cleaning flows toward the optical surface.</p><Figure id="soft-nozzle" quiet /></div>
  </div>
  <div class="surgery-control">
    <h3>Perception-Guided Cleaning Control</h3>
    <p class="surgery-sublead">We investigate a feedback-control architecture that estimates visual obstruction and coordinates robotic wiping with directed cleaning flows.</p>
    <Figure id="cleaning-control" quiet />
  </div>
</section>

<section class="project-section surgery-section" id="visual-ai" aria-labelledby="visual-ai-title">
  <h2 id="visual-ai-title">03 — Agentic AI for Surgical Robotics</h2>
  <h3 class="surgery-subhead">Toward Perception, Reasoning, and Intelligent Action</h3>
  <p class="surgery-lead">Our long-term research goal is to develop surgeon-supervised robotic agents capable of understanding surgical environments, reasoning about procedural tasks, and coordinating adaptive robotic actions.</p>
  <p class="surgery-lead">As a foundation for this vision, we investigate AI-based surgical scene understanding, including instrument localization, instrument–tissue interaction recognition, and spatial feedback in simulated laparoscopic environments.</p>
  <div class="surgery-wide"><Figure id="ai-pipeline" quiet /></div>
  <ol class="surgery-pipeline">
    {understanding.map(item => <li><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.text}</p></li>)}
  </ol>
  <div class="surgery-spatial">
    <h3>From Visual Understanding to Intelligent Assistance</h3>
    <p class="surgery-sublead">Two-dimensional surgical views may obscure critical spatial relationships between instruments and tissues. Our simulation-based research demonstrates how 3D contextual feedback can distinguish visually similar but physically different interactions.</p>
    <Figure id="spatial-feedback" quiet />
    <p class="surgery-view-tags" aria-hidden="true"><span>2D view</span><span>3D context</span></p>
    <p class="surgery-caption">Top: contact. Bottom: missed contact.</p>
    <p class="surgery-sublead surgery-closing">These capabilities represent foundational steps toward agentic surgical robots that connect perception, reasoning, planning, and physical action under surgeon supervision.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="project-section surgery-section surgery-vision" id="research-vision" aria-labelledby="research-vision-title">
  <h2 id="research-vision-title">Research Vision</h2>
  <p class="surgery-lead">We envision a new generation of intelligent surgical robotic systems that combine bioinspired physical mechanisms with agentic AI to enable context-aware, adaptive, and surgeon-centered assistance during minimally invasive procedures.</p>
</section>

<section class="project-section surgery-citation" id="publications" aria-labelledby="publications-title">
  <h2 id="publications-title">Related Publications</h2>
  {publications.map(group => <>
    <h3 class="surgery-pub-group">{group.group}</h3>
    <ol class="surgery-pub-list">
      {group.papers.map(paper => <li>
        <p class="surgery-pub-title"><a href={`https://doi.org/${paper.doi}`}>{paper.title}</a></p>
        <p class="surgery-pub-authors">{paper.authors}</p>
        <p class="surgery-pub-venue"><span>{paper.venue}</span> · {paper.year}</p>
      </li>)}
    </ol>
  </>)}
  <p class="surgery-fine">Links open the publisher’s DOI record. AI figures are reproduced from the <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.02233">arXiv version</a> under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. <a href={asset("accessibility/#surgery-media")}>Accessibility &amp; media credits</a> · <a href={asset("#lab-projects")}>All projects</a> · <a href="#main">Back to top ↑</a></p>
</section>
